{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-trap-semantics","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The EXIT-trap fallback design in tally-plan-review.sh relies on $? being stable inside Bash EXIT traps under set -e — subtle semantics the static correctness panel rarely probes at this depth without a targeted directive.","prompt_body":"Read `skills/design/scripts/tally-plan-review.sh` and verify that `$?` inside the cleanup EXIT trap reliably reflects the script's actual exit code under every termination path: explicit `exit N` calls from within functions, implicit `set -e` exits triggered by pipeline failures, and any subshell exits. Confirm that the guard variable `_tally_status_emitted` will be in scope and mutable from inside the EXIT trap handler — pay attention to whether any function that sets it uses `local`, which would hide the assignment from the trap's enclosing scope. Check whether the conditional `$? != 0` check in the cleanup trap could fire on a zero-exit path if the trap body itself contains a command that fails before the condition is evaluated. Verify that the proposed 'set guard first, then emit' ordering is sufficient to prevent double-emission if a signal or `set -e` fires between the guard assignment and the emit call. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"refactor-equivalence","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The plan claims the helper extraction is byte-identical to today on the happy path but does not pin the current emit order or exact predicate expressions — a gap the static panel won't catch without reading dispatch-plan-voters.sh directly.","prompt_body":"Read `scripts/dispatch-plan-voters.sh` lines approximately 198-236 and verify that the three proposed helper functions in `scripts/lib-voter-coverage.sh` faithfully reproduce the current emit order, condition expressions, and loop variable semantics. Specifically check whether the degraded-warning predicate in the plan (`effective_judges < expected_judges`) matches the actual inline logic and whether `expected_judges` is derived from a constant, a counted slot list, or a passed argument — the plan does not define it. Confirm the plan's function signatures carry all values currently read from script-level variables, because any variable the inline code reads from outer scope must become an explicit argument to preserve per-round routing safety. Check whether the plan's `voter_coverage_emit_voter_kvs` description accounts for all four KVs emitted per voter today and whether the emit ordering within each slot is preserved. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
